EDGECLAW(1)                General Commands Manual                EDGECLAW(1)

NAME
     edgeclaw — AI-powered personal assistant agent

SYNOPSIS
     edgeclaw [--setup] [--sandbox] [--help] [--version]

DESCRIPTION
     EdgeClaw is a command-line AI assistant offering shell-like task manage-
     ment.  It supports multi-turn conversations, tool calling, and Unix-
     inspired process control for orchestrating multiple parallel agent tasks.

OPTIONS
     -s, --setup    Run initial setup wizard and exit.
     -S, --sandbox  Enable filesystem sandbox for tool execution.
     -h, --help     Display help and exit.
     -V, --version  Output version information and exit.

GETTING STARTED
           $ edgeclaw
           > hello
           Welcome! How can I help?

     Type and press Enter.  The conversation is saved automatically per
     session under workspace/<session_id>/.

TASK MODEL
     EdgeClaw uses a three-layer Unix-like process model:

     Task     — A conversation with its own state, output, and lifecycle.
                Shares all history with sibling tasks in the same group.
                Like a Unix thread sharing the process address space.

     Group    — An isolated execution container.  All tasks in a group
                share the full conversation history automatically.
                Cross-group communication requires explicit /task chain.
                Like a Unix process (private memory).

     Session  — A login session containing multiple groups.  History is
                stored per-session on disk.  Like a Unix login session.

TASK STATES
       IDLE → WAITING_DEPS → WAITING_INPUT → LLM_PROCESSING
         → TOOL_CALLING → SUSPENDED → COMPLETED / CANCELLED / ERROR

     - COMPLETED tasks persist until reclaimed with /task join.
     - SUSPENDED tasks can be resumed.
     - CANCELLED / ERROR tasks can also be reclaimed with /task join.

SHELL-LIKE JOB CONTROL
     Shell            EdgeClaw
     ─────────────────────────────────────────
     cmd &            /task new "desc" --bg
     jobs             /task list
     fg %1            /task fg <task_id>
     bg %1            /task bg
     kill %1          /task stop <task_id>
     Ctrl+Z           /task suspend <task_id>
     fg / bg (resume) /task resume <task_id>
     cmd1 | cmd2      /task chain <src> <dst>
     pthread_join()   /task join <task_id>
     fork()           /task fork <parent> <desc>
     setsid           /task new <desc>

COMMAND REFERENCE
     Session
     /new          Start a new session (clear turns, trigger compress).
     /stop         Cancel all active tasks in foreground group.
     /help         Show help message.
     /exit         Exit edgeclaw.

     Task creation
     /task new <desc>                  Create new group + task (foreground).
     /task new <desc> --bg             Create new group + task (background).
     /task new <desc> --group <gid>    Create task inside an existing group.
     /task fork <pid> <desc>           Fork a child task in parent's group,
                                        inheriting fork-point context.
     /task fork <pid> <desc> --prompt <name>
                                       Fork with a subagent prompt.

     Task inspection
     /task list               List all groups and tasks (markdown tree).
     /task status [tid]       Show task details (state, output, deps).
     /task output <tid>       Show only the task's output text.
     /task groups             List all process groups.

     Foreground / active
     /task fg <tid>           Bring task's group to foreground.
     /task bg                 Move current foreground group to background.
     /task switch <tid>       Set active task within the foreground group.
                              Only the active task outputs to the terminal.

     Task control
     /task stop <tid>         Cancel a task (SIGTERM — notifies deps).
     /task stop --force <id>  Force-kill (SIGKILL — no dep notification).
     /task stop --group <id>  Cancel all tasks in a process group.
     /task suspend <tid>      Pause a task (like Ctrl+Z).
     /task resume <tid>       Resume a suspended task.
     /task join <tid>         If COMPLETED: show output and reclaim.
                              If RUNNING: shows how to chain/wait.
     /task prompt <tid> <name>
                              Set a subagent prompt for the task.

     Pipelines
     /task chain <src> <dst>  Make dst wait for src.  When src completes,
                              its output (final LLM reply) is injected into
                              dst's context as a system message.
                              Like: src | dst

CONTEXT SHARING
     Within a group: all tasks automatically share the full conversation
     history (every turn with all messages, tool calls, and thinking).

     Cross-group: use /task chain to inject a source task's output into a
     destination task's LLM context when the source completes.

     Forked tasks: a child task inherits the group's history up to the
     fork point (not the parent's subsequent messages).  Use /task chain
     to pass the parent's final result to the child.

OUTPUT
     Only the session's active task outputs to the terminal.  Other tasks
     in the same group complete silently; use /task join to retrieve their
     output.  Use /task switch to change the active task.

WORKFLOW EXAMPLES
     Simple conversation
           $ edgeclaw
           > hello
           Welcome! How can I help?
           > write a Python HTTP server
           Here's a simple HTTP server: ...

     Background analysis
           > /task new "analyze logs" --bg
           [task] created task-1 (group task-1) in background
           > /task new "analyze frontend" --bg
           > /task list
           - [fg] task-1 (2)
               - [L] task-1  WAITING_INPUT  analyze logs
               - [M] task-2  WAITING_INPUT  analyze frontend

     Pipeline (cross-group)
           > /task new "summary"
           > /task chain task-1 task-0     ← task-0 waits for task-1
           > /task chain task-2 task-0     ← task-0 waits for both
           ... task-1, task-2 complete ...
           > /task switch task-0
           > please summarize both results
           → LLM sees task-1 and task-2 outputs injected

     Fork + subagent
           > /task fork task-1 "review" --prompt code-reviewer
           [fork] created fork-xx from task-1 (group task-1)
           > /task switch fork-xx
           > review src/agent.c
           → child sees parent's group history up to fork point

     Join and reclaim
           > /task join task-1
           [task-1 output]: "Analysis complete: 3 issues found..."
           → task-1 reclaimed and removed

     Suspend and resume
           > /task suspend task-3
           task task-3 suspended
           > /task resume task-3
           task task-3 resumed

FILES
     ~/.edgeclaw/                        EdgeClaw home directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/config.json             Configuration (API key, model).
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/              Workspace root for tools and files.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/        Per-session directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/history.jsonl
                                         Session conversation history.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/history_info.json
                                         Session metadata.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/memory/MEMORY.md
                                         Agent memory file.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/skills/       Custom skill SKILL.md files.

